# predicates: spills(X) = spills a lot of food on clothing; tidy(X) = notably tidy; cleanly(X); values_order(X) = values order and spotlessness; family_order(X) = family prioritizes order and spotlessness; explorer(X) = clumsy foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try
spills(X) => not tidy(X). @ "If people spill a lot of food on their clothing, then they are not notably tidy."
explorer(X) => spills(X). @ "All clumsy foodies who go out frequently to find new food restaurants to try are people who spill a lot of food on their clothing."
cleanly(X) => tidy(X). @ "If people are cleanly, then they are notably tidy."
values_order(X) => cleanly(X). @ "If people value order and spotlessness, then they are cleanly."
family_order(X) => values_order(X). @ "All people whose families prioritize order and spotlessness themselves value order and spotlessness."
# Peter: (spills and cleanly) or (not spills and not cleanly), i.e. spills iff cleanly
spills(peter) => cleanly(peter). @ "Peter is either a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and is cleanly, or a person who does not spill a lot of food on his clothing and is not cleanly"
cleanly(peter) => spills(peter). @ "Peter is either a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and is cleanly, or a person who does not spill a lot of food on his clothing and is not cleanly"
not spills(peter) => not cleanly(peter). @ "Peter is either a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and is cleanly, or a person who does not spill a lot of food on his clothing and is not cleanly"
not cleanly(peter) => not spills(peter). @ "Peter is either a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and is cleanly, or a person who does not spill a lot of food on his clothing and is not cleanly"
# helper: the statement to verify is (both) or (neither)
* explorer(X) & family_order(X) => target(X).
* not explorer(X) & not family_order(X) => target(X).
? target(peter).
